findings.recommendation:
Add a token format assertion at the entry point of each public method, or URL-encode the token before HTML-escaping it. Preferred approach — assert the contract:

private static final java.util.regex.Pattern BASE64URL = java.util.regex.Pattern.compile("^[A-Za-z0-9_-]+$");

public void sendVerificationEmail(String to, String name, String token) {
    if (!BASE64URL.matcher(token).matches()) {
        throw new IllegalArgumentException("Token must be base64url-safe");
    }
    String body = buildVerificationBody(name, token);
    if (body.isEmpty()) return;
    sendAsync(to, "Підтвердіть email", body);
}

Alternatively, URL-encode first, then HTML-escape:

private static String urlEncodeAndHtmlEscape(String value) {
    return htmlEscape(java.net.URLEncoder.encode(value, StandardCharsets.UTF_8));
}

The assertion is simpler and more explicit about the design contract.

Add a dedicated test for token escaping in the email body:

@Test
void buildVerificationBody_htmlEscapesTokenInHref() {
    String body = emailService.buildVerificationBody("Alice", "\"onload=alert(1) x=\"");
    assertThat(body).contains("&quot;onload");
    assertThat(body).doesNotContain("\"onload=alert");
}

Similarly add a test for buildPasswordResetBody with a malicious token.

Add a null guard in htmlEscape:

static String htmlEscape(String input) {
    if (input == null) return "";
    return input
            .replace("&", "&amp;")
            .replace("<", "&lt;")
            .replace(">", "&gt;")
            .replace("\"", "&quot;")
            .replace("'", "&#39;");
}

Alternatively, add Objects.requireNonNull checks at the top of each public method to fail fast with a descriptive message.

Validate the from address format at construction time using InternetAddress:

public EmailService(JavaMailSender javaMailSender,
                    @Value("${app.mail.from}") String from,
                    @Value("${app.url}") String appUrl) throws AddressException {
    new InternetAddress(from).validate(); // throws AddressException on malformed/CRLF
    this.javaMailSender = javaMailSender;
    this.from = from;
    this.appUrl = appUrl;
}

InternetAddress.validate() rejects addresses containing unencoded CRLF, preventing header injection. Wrap the AddressException as IllegalStateException so Spring fails fast at startup.
